The latest news about the Symfony framework includes several updates and developments. Maintenance versions 6.4.46, 7.4.19, and 8.1.7 were released, and the "New in Symfony 8.2" blog series has begun, highlighting new features and improvements in the upcoming version 8.2, which will move configuration and services of most components out of FrameworkBundle and into a bundle shipped by each component^1^. Additionally, a new Symfony Security: The Basics course was published by SymfonyCasts, and there are only a few days left to purchase regular tickets for SymfonyCon Warsaw 2026, which will feature talks on topics such as malware detection, immutable tags, and strict dependency policies^1^. 

Symfony 7.4.5 was also released with a security fix (CVE-2026-24739) and numerous bug fixes, and Symfony 6.4.33 was released with an upgrade guide and upgrade reports, including fixes for streams with CurlHttpClient, DocBlock resolution, PdoSessionHandler charset, AsyncResponse decoration, and Windows MSYS escaping^6^. 

Furthermore, Symfony has introduced standalone bundles for framework features like MessengerBundle, HttpClientBundle, and PropertyInfoBundle, and has added Docker-style console subcommands and expanded OAuth2 and OpenID Connect support. The workshop lineup and some talks for SymfonyCon Warsaw 2026 have also been announced^9^.
